dante 1.4.4 libdsocks: getsockname(2) on a non-blocking connect in progress
must not wait for SIGIO

Applies on top of the other dante-*.patch files (it only touches
Rgetsockname() in lib/Rgetsockname.c).

For a socket whose non-blocking connect through the proxy is still in
progress, Rgetsockname() waited with sigsuspend() for the SIGIO that
completes it, then retried. SIGIO goes to any thread of the program, so in a
threaded program the waiting thread may never get it and hangs for good.
libcurl calls getsockname() right after connect(): with transmission every
HTTP tracker announce stayed "in progress" for as long as the daemon ran
(its web thread sat in sigsuspend()). It now answers like the kernel does
for a connect in progress, with the descriptor's current local address; the
proxy's address for us is not known yet at that point anyway.

Test: test/getsockcheck.c (via test/run.sh).

--- a/lib/Rgetsockname.c
+++ b/lib/Rgetsockname.c
@@ -141,34 +141,23 @@
 
             if (socksfd.state.inprogress) { /* non-blocking connect. */
                /*
-                * this is bad.  We don't know what address the socks server
-                * will use on our behalf yet.  Lets wait for a SIGIO
-                * and then retry, unless client is blocking that signal,
-                * then we can only hope the client will retry on ENOBUFS,
-                * but we are probably screwed anyway.
-               */
-               if (sigismember(&oset, SIGIO)) {
-                  swarnx("%s: SIGIO is being blocked by client", function);
-
-                  if (sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK, &oset, NULL) != 0) {
-                     swarn("%s: sigprocmask()", function);
-                     return -1;
-                  }
-
-                  errno = ENOBUFS;
-                  return -1;
-               }
-
-
-               slog(LOG_DEBUG, "%s: waiting for signal from child", function);
-               sigsuspend(&oset); /* wait for sigchld. */
-
-               if (sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK, &oset, NULL) != 0) {
+                * We do not know yet what address the socks server will use
+                * on our behalf.  This used to wait here with sigsuspend()
+                * for the SIGIO that completes the connect, but SIGIO goes
+                * to any thread of the program: in a threaded program that
+                * one may never come to this thread, which then hangs (e.g.
+                * libcurl calls getsockname() right after connect()).
+                * Answer like the kernel does for a connect in progress:
+                * with the descriptor's current local address.
+                */
+               if (sigprocmask(SIG_SETMASK, &oset, NULL) != 0)
                   swarn("%s: sigprocmask()", function);
-                  return -1;
-               }
 
-               return Rgetsockname(s, name, namelen);
+               slog(LOG_DEBUG, "%s: fd %d: connect in progress, returning "
+                               "its current local address",
+                    function, s);
+
+               return getsockname(s, name, namelen);
             }
 
             if (sigprocmask(SIG_SETMASK, &oset, NULL) != 0)
